    "updated_at": "2016-02-04T17:15:32Z",
    "body": "Working on getting this in now. Overall quite excited. I wanted to let you know that we did indeed take your advice on the build script getting less from the internet; recent updates to html-build make it only fetch two files (w3cbugs.csv and caniuse.json) with the rest generated ahead of time. And there's talk of how to reduce those two away too (although you can already do so with the `-n` switch).\n\nAs for the separate repos, I think the main value there is being able to get a pure version history (\"changelog\") for HTML by looking at the whatwg/html commits, instead of getting all our build tooling work in there too. A lot of people consume the version history here besides just the editors. So I'm still pretty happy with that split.\n\nA couple questions:\n- I am getting \"Warning: ipv4 forwarding is disabled. Networking will not work.\" plus then all the package downloads fail. It seems like this is a system-level configuration, not really docker-related, and so I can fix it for my machine with some ease. But do we anticipate others having the same problem? Is there a chance this changed in your latest work? I don't remember having this problem before when testing your previous builds.\n- If we eventually wanted to get this published to dockerhub, what would end up changing? Would it be best to put this work into a separate repo, like Node.js does?? Would it enable us to get rid of the .dockerignore and Dockerfile, and tell people to run a less cryptic command? Or is it mostly for people building other dockerfiles on top of this one?\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
