    "created_at": "2016-02-04T18:35:31Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-02-04T18:35:31Z",
    "body": "> Overall quite excited. I wanted to let you know that we did indeed take your advice on the build script getting less from the internet; recent updates to html-build make it only fetch two files\n\nGreat! Not sure if the Dockerfile will need to change beyond just bumping the githash for the html-build clone.\n\n> As for the separate repos, I think the main value there is being able to get a pure version history (\"changelog\") for HTML by looking at the whatwg/html commits, instead of getting all our build tooling work in there too. A lot of people consume the version history here besides just the editors. So I'm still pretty happy with that split.\n\nFair enough.\n\n> I am getting \"Warning: ipv4 forwarding is disabled. Networking will not work.\"\n\nNever seen this error before. Nothing in the way the docker images are built would be causing that. It sounds to me like a system/docker setup config issue. I wouldn't expect this to affect lots of users unless something has broken in recent docker installs or use that I am not aware of.\n\n> If we eventually wanted to get this published to dockerhub, what would end up changing?\n\nDepends what your intent is by publishing. The reason to publish publicly to docker hub are usually just two:\n- use as a base image for building images on top (because it provides some base software you want to build using)\n- use as a service image: nginx, mongo, etc (because it provides some API or software your things depend on)\n\nYou could say that posting on docker hub also makes it easy to share the final build artifact so users can run their own copy of the spec server but this may not be so interesting for now or relevant to you. I suppose another reason to post to docker hub is to get some sort of automatic deployments going that pull the image from docker hub and deploy it on your server.\n\nI would not separate out the docker parts into yet another repo for now. All you will do is introduce more work for anyone who wants to get your code, build it, and see the build results. I would say that for node it makes more sense because they are end package agnostic but for your requirement/request the Dockerfile is actually part of the development/testing experience for this repo and thus is better served in one place.\n\nAgain this revisits the idea that you could publish base images to docker-hub with your build tools or whatnot if it becomes useful to do so for sharing across many repos.\n\n> Would it enable us to get rid of the .dockerignore and Dockerfile, and tell people to run a less cryptic command?\n\nNot really cause the whole point I gathered was to use docker to see what your output looks like and for that you need to run the docker build and then run the docker image. If the command is cryptic just add scripts to make it easier: `whatwg-html-build`, `whatwg-html-run` or somesuch :)\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
